Rust 1.100 — `std::sync::LazyLock` is available (stable since 1.80). So I can use `LazyLock<Mutex<HashSet<String>>>` without external crates.

For randomness without the `rand` crate: I could use `SystemTime` and a simple PRNG (PCG or xorshift) seeded from entropy. But to make it "not follow a predictable sequence" — the tests only check pattern and uniqueness, but the spec says random. A PRNG seeded from SystemTime nanoseconds + address entropy is sufficient for this exercise.

Actually there's an even simpler approach: enumerate all 676,000 names and shuffle them? That's overkill. Better: a global xorshift/PCG32 PRNG seeded from SystemTime, generating random names and checking against the used set.

Let me write it:

```rust
use std::collections::HashSet;
use std::sync::{LazyLock, Mutex};

/// All names currently assigned to robots (including retired ones).
static USED_NAMES: LazyLock<Mutex<HashSet<String>>> =
    LazyLock::new(|| Mutex::new(HashSet::new()));

pub struct Robot {
    name: String,
}

impl Robot {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self { name: Self::generate_name() }
    }

    pub fn name(&self) -> &str {
        &self.name
    }

    pub fn reset_name(&mut self) {
        self.name = Self::generate_name();
    }

    fn generate_name() -> String {
        let mut used = USED_NAMES.lock().unwrap();
        loop {
            let name = random_name();
            if used.insert(name.clone()) {
                return name;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Wait — should the old name be removed from the set on reset? If reset removes the old name, then another robot could get the same name as the old one... but the old one is no longer in use, so that's fine. But if we keep it, we'll exhaust the 676,000 names eventually. For the tests either works. Simpler: keep it (don't remove on reset). Actually, the spec says "every existing robot has a unique name" — keeping retired names in the set is safe and simple.

Random name generation: I need a PRNG. Let me implement a simple PCG32 or xorshift64* seeded from SystemTime.

```rust
use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};

struct Rng {
    state: u64,
}

impl Rng {
    fn new() -> Self {
        let nanos = SystemTime::now()
            .duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH)
            .map(|d| d.as_nanos() as u64)
            .unwrap_or(0x9E3779B97F4A7C15);
        Self { state: nanos ^ 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15 }
    }

    fn next_u64(&mut self) -> u64 {
        // xorshift64*
        let mut x = self.state;
        x ^= x >> 12;
        x ^= x << 25;
        x ^= x >> 27;
        self.state = x;
        x.wrapping_mul(0x2545F4914F6CDD1D)
    }
}
```

But I need a global RNG — another LazyLock<Mutex<Rng>>. And name generation:

```rust
fn random_name(rng: &mut Rng) -> String {
    let mut s = String::with_capacity(5);
    for _ in 0..2 {
        s.push((b'A' + rng.next_u64() % 26) as char);
    }
    for _ in 0..3 {
        s.push((b'0' + rng.next_u64() % 10) as char);
    }
    s
}
```

Note: xorshift64* fails if the state is 0 — the seed from SystemTime nanoseconds won't be 0, but just in case let me guard against it.

Alternatively, use SystemTime directly for each name? No — nanosecond resolution isn't guaranteed, and consecutive calls could return the same value. A PRNG is better.

The tests are run in parallel threads, so the Mutex around the RNG and the used set handles that.

